# Bracha 1987, "Asynchronous Byzantine Agreement Protocols"
# Information and Computation 75, 130-143 (1987)
#
# Figure 1 - Reliable Broadcast Primitive
#
# The reliable broadcast primitive is given by six rules.  Each
# rule is of the form: upon receiving a particular kind of
# message, if a particular state holds, take a particular action.
#
# Each correct process p_i maintains, per broadcast instance:
#   "have echoed"      - has p_i sent (echo, v)?
#   "have sent ready"  - has p_i sent (ready, v)?
# and counts the (echo, v) and (ready, v) messages received from
# distinct senders, separately for each value v.
#
# The decision tables below answer the three questions a process
# faces upon receiving any message: should I send (echo, v),
# send (ready, v), or accept(v)?  Each row is taken directly
# from a rule of the paper.  A blank cell means the rule's
# outcome does not turn on that input here -- either because the
# rule genuinely ignores it, or because a chained input already
# fixes its value (see the chaining note below).
#
# Names appearing in this file are written in the paper's
# notation - embedded commas, parentheses, and inequality forms
# included.  The file bracha87Fig1ToC.dtc maps each name and
# value to a C-friendly identifier and to the C expression
# evaluated at runtime.
#
# Inputs (the situation when the message is processed; v denotes
# the value carried by the message):
#   "kind of message"            { (initial, v), (echo, v), (ready, v) }
#   "have echoed"                { yes, no }
#   "have sent ready"            { yes, no }
#   "echo-count(v) > (n+t)/2"    { yes, no }
#   "ready-count(v) >= t+1"      { yes, no }
#   "ready-count(v) >= 2t+1"     { yes, no }
#   "have accepted"              { yes, no }
#
# Outputs (the actions the process may take):
#   "send (echo, v)"             { yes, no }
#   "send (ready, v)"            { yes, no }
#   "accept(v)"                  { yes, no }
#
# "have accepted" is an input of the paper rules that Fig 1 does not
# write down: a step program ends at accept(v), so it accepts once.
# The row that says so is on the accept table below, and it is what
# lets every arrival -- a duplicate, a post-accept ready -- run this
# dispatch: the BPR rules further down consume messages the paper is
# finished with, and no paper output fires twice.
#
# The figure is a sequence of STEPS, not a set of independent
# rules: Bracha87.txt reads "Three paths into echo (step 1), two
# paths into ready (step 2), one path to accept (step 3)."  A
# step program waits on what it has received so far, so the
# echoes that carry step 1 are still there when step 2 is
# evaluated, and step 2's "I have echoed" is satisfied by the
# echo step 1 just sent.  Evaluating the three tables against
# the state the message ARRIVED with loses exactly that, and
# loses it only where a step fires on the message that carries
# its own precondition.
#
# So the tables are CHAINED: the ready tables take "send (echo,
# v)" as an input and the accept table takes "send (ready, v)",
# which is how a table reads the state its predecessor leaves.
# That is what lets every rule below transcribe from the figure
# literally, with none rewritten to carry a predecessor's
# effect.  DTC resolves the chain when it compiles the dispatch,
# the same mechanism the BPR rules below use to consume the
# paper outputs.
#
# The dependencies are one-directional -- no echo rule reads a
# ready output and no ready rule reads accept -- so the chain is
# a DAG and DTC orders it.
#
# Cells covering states unreachable by the rules' invariants
# (e.g. "have echoed = no" with "have sent ready = yes", which
# Rules 4 and 5 prevent) are coded as no-action rows so that
# every input combination resolves.

# ===== send (echo, v) =====

# Rule 1.  Upon receiving (initial, v) from the initiator,
# if I have not echoed, send (echo, v).
@"send (echo, v)","kind of message","have echoed"
yes,"(initial, v)",no
no,"(initial, v)",yes

# Rule 2.  Upon receiving more than (n+t)/2 (echo, v) messages,
# if I have not echoed, send (echo, v).
@"send (echo, v)","kind of message","have echoed","echo-count(v) > (n+t)/2"
yes,"(echo, v)",no,yes
no,"(echo, v)",no,no
no,"(echo, v)",yes,

# Rule 3.  Upon receiving t+1 (ready, v) messages, if I have not
# echoed, send (echo, v).
@"send (echo, v)","kind of message","have echoed","ready-count(v) >= t+1"
yes,"(ready, v)",no,yes
no,"(ready, v)",no,no
no,"(ready, v)",yes,

# ===== send (ready, v) =====

# No rule sends ready upon receiving (initial, v).
@"send (ready, v)","kind of message"
no,"(initial, v)"

# Rule 4.  Upon receiving more than (n+t)/2 (echo, v) messages,
# if I have echoed but not sent ready, send (ready, v).
#
# "I have echoed" is read AFTER the echo rules have run on this
# same message, which is what "send (echo, v)" carries: on an
# (echo, v) message the only echo rule that can fire is Rule 2,
# and Rule 2 firing means "have echoed" was no and the echo
# threshold was met, so both are don't-care once it has fired.
@"send (ready, v)","kind of message","send (echo, v)","have echoed","have sent ready","echo-count(v) > (n+t)/2"
yes,"(echo, v)",yes,,no,
no,"(echo, v)",yes,,yes,
yes,"(echo, v)",no,yes,no,yes
no,"(echo, v)",no,yes,no,no
no,"(echo, v)",no,yes,yes,
no,"(echo, v)",no,no,,

# Rule 5.  Upon receiving t+1 (ready, v) messages, if I have
# echoed but not sent ready, send (ready, v).
#
# Same chaining as Rule 4, and it is what carries Rule 3 into
# Rule 5: on a (ready, v) message that finds "have echoed" no
# with t+1 readys, Rule 3 fires, and Rule 5 reads that echo
# rather than the flag the message arrived on.
@"send (ready, v)","kind of message","send (echo, v)","have echoed","have sent ready","ready-count(v) >= t+1"
yes,"(ready, v)",yes,,no,
no,"(ready, v)",yes,,yes,
yes,"(ready, v)",no,yes,no,yes
no,"(ready, v)",no,yes,no,no
no,"(ready, v)",no,yes,yes,
no,"(ready, v)",no,no,,

# ===== accept(v) =====

# Rule 6.  Upon receiving 2t+1 (ready, v) messages, if I have
# sent ready, accept(v).
#
# Bracha87.txt renders the accept row with a "readySent"
# conjunct -- the extract's own condition-column rendering of a
# step program, in a figure whose scan it calls garbled, so the
# column is the extract's shape rather than a certainty of the
# paper's.  It is read after the ready rules have run on this
# same message, which is what "send (ready, v)" carries.  2t+1 readys implies t+1, so a
# process that had not sent ready fires Rule 5 here and accepts
# on the same message -- the conjunct never withholds an accept,
# and stating it keeps the row the figure's.
#
# Once: a process that has accepted accepts no more -- the step
# program has ended.  Stated as a row so that arrivals after accept
# (recorded for the BPR sets, and consumed by the BPR rules below)
# fire nothing here.
@"accept(v)","kind of message","send (ready, v)","have sent ready","ready-count(v) >= 2t+1","have accepted"
yes,"(ready, v)",yes,,yes,no
no,"(ready, v)",yes,,no,
no,"(ready, v)",yes,,,yes
yes,"(ready, v)",no,yes,yes,no
no,"(ready, v)",no,yes,no,
no,"(ready, v)",no,yes,,yes
no,"(ready, v)",no,no,,
no,"(initial, v)",,,,
no,"(echo, v)",,,,

# ===========================================================
# Bracha Phase Retry (BPR)
# ===========================================================
#
# Bracha's correctness proof presumes a reliable point-to-point
# channel between honest parties.  Over a fair-loss datagram
# network that assumption is not satisfied.  BPR joins the six
# paper rules above with rules that restore eventual delivery by
# retrying the broadcast actions an instance has sent, whenever
# the application's retry ticks.  No application-layer bookkeeping
# is needed: retry state is intrinsic to Bracha's own sent flags
# (echoed, rdSent), an "am initiator" bit that names this process
# as the broadcast initiator, and the per-sender bitmaps the
# instance already keeps -- who echoed (the INITIAL retire and
# mask), who readied (the ECHO mask), who announced an accept and
# who is armed (the READY retire and mask).
#
# Justification: end-to-end argument (Saltzer/Reed/Clark 1984;
# see SRC84.txt).  The "still owed" predicate lives at the Bracha
# endpoint -- the sent flags and the per-sender bitmaps -- so the
# retransmission function is placed at the endpoint that holds
# the knowledge needed to implement it correctly.  A separate
# stubborn-link layer below would have to be told by Bracha when
# to retire (folding the function back here anyway) and would lack
# Bracha's information per SRC84 P3(b).
#
# Composition.  BPR rules consume the paper outputs as inputs.
# DTC chains the dispatch: Bracha rules compute first; BPR rules
# observe Bracha's decisions and the sent-state flags, then
# output distinct "retry" outputs.  The chain is one-directional -
# BPR cannot affect Bracha decisions - so an audit of paper
# fidelity reads only the paper section above without having to
# consider any BPR row.
#
# Trigger.  The application calls bracha87Fig1Bpr on an instance
# whenever its own retry cadence advances.  No wall-clock
# predicate appears anywhere; the application's tick IS the
# event, so asynchrony is preserved.  In silent periods the
# application omits the call and the protocol stays silent.
#
# One dispatch, two entry points.  bracha87Fig1Input runs it on
# every arriving message and applies the paper outputs and the
# annotation outputs; bracha87Fig1Bpr runs it on a tick and
# applies the retry outputs.  Each entry discards the other's
# outputs.  The inputs it feeds from state are the ones its own
# outputs read; the rest it feeds a fixed value (Bpr's "kind of
# message" is (initial, v) with the thresholds unmet, ACCEPTED
# absent and RECEIVED present; Input's "am initiator", "all echoed" and
# "READY mask covers all n" are "no"), and the discarded outputs
# may fire on them -- an un-echoed initiator's tick fires Rule 1,
# and drops it.  What makes that harmless is that nothing an entry
# reads moves with a value it fed by fiat -- a fact of the rows,
# not of the table's shape: the echo and ready retries chain on the send rules,
# which read the kind of message Bpr fixes, and they stay constant
# because a retry is "no" whenever its send fires.  So it is
# checked, not derived: test_predicates enumerates both entries'
# input spaces against the snippet and requires it.  The rules
# are written once, in the paper's and
# BPR.md's vocabulary, and dtc resolves the chains across both
# groups.
#
# Retirement (minimal retry).  Each retry stops at the soonest
# point it is provably no longer owed to any correct process
# (BPR.md, Retirement).  INITIAL and ECHO are bootstrap-only:
# they exist to drive t+1 correct processes to send (ready, v),
# after which Bracha's ready-amplification (rdCnt >= t+1 -> send
# ready) is self-sustaining and consumes neither.  "have
# accepted" is the local witness this has passed (>= t+1 of the
# 2t+1 readys are correct, retried until every process has
# accepted and holds the retrier's accept), so INITIAL and ECHO
# retire at "have accepted" yes.  INITIAL also retires at "all
# echoed" -- it induces only echoes, so once every process has
# echoed there is nothing left for it to induce.  (ready, v) is
# NOT dropped on accepted: it is exactly what the amplification
# tail consumes -- an accepted process still owes its ready to
# processes under 2t+1, and retry is the only delivery mechanism
# under loss.  It retires only on remote evidence, per process
# and whole-action: it is suppressed to each process for which
# the remote pair of facts holds -- accepted, and holding this
# instance's accept (the ACCEPTED and RECEIVED annotations riding
# the READY already being retried) -- and the retry falls silent
# when that evidence covers all n ("READY suppress mask covers all
# n"): quiescence, the owing ending.  The forbidden weaker gates
# -- INITIAL at merely echoed, READY at local accept -- are what
# the rows below do not say (README Implementation Notes 10, 11).
#
# The annotation exchange (BPR.md, Suppression and the
# Announcements).  A (ready, v) carries two facts beside its value:
#   - the ACCEPTED annotation: its sender has accepted this
#     instance and consumes no further ready from us -- record
#     the sender as accepted (bracha87Fig1ProcessAccepted; drives
#     the READY suppress mask and the all-n retire).  The record
#     follows the message's own ready record, taken in C before
#     this dispatch, so the accepted set stays a subset of the
#     readied set.  Self is a sender like any other: its own
#     accept arrives on its own hand-back carrying ACCEPTED.
#   - the ABSENCE of the RECEIVED annotation: its sender has not
#     recorded our accept, or it would have suppressed us instead
#     of sending -- arm one marked re-send toward the sender
#     (bracha87Fig1ProcessResend), once we have an accept to
#     announce.  "Once we have" is read AFTER the accept rule has
#     run on this same message, which is what "accept(v)" carries:
#     the READY that accepts this instance and arrives unmarked
#     arms its sender in the same dispatch.  Before we have
#     accepted nothing is owed; the sender's unmarked READY keeps
#     arriving until we do, and its first arrival after our accept
#     is the one that arms.
# INITIAL and ECHO carry no annotation and fire neither.  A
# duplicate (ready, v) -- an unmarked re-send is one -- reaches
# these rules because the dispatch runs on every arrival; that is
# why "have accepted" is a paper input above rather than a C
# early return.
#
# Additional inputs introduced by BPR (all read from the instance;
# bridged in bracha87Fig1ToC.dtc):
#   "am initiator"                   { yes, no }
#   "held"                           { yes, no }   the initiator withholds (initial, v)
#   "all echoed"                     { yes, no }   echo senders == n
#   "READY suppress mask covers all n" { yes, no }
#   "carries ACCEPTED"               { yes, no }   read on (ready, v)
#   "carries RECEIVED"               { yes, no }   read on (ready, v)
#
# Additional outputs introduced by BPR:
#   "retry (initial, v)"             { yes, no }
#   "retry (echo, v)"                { yes, no }
#   "retry (ready, v)"               { yes, no }
#   "record sender accepted"         { yes, no }
#   "arm re-send to sender"          { yes, no }

# ===== retry (initial, v) =====
#
# The initiator's INITIAL, until accepted or all-echoed.  Not
# gated on "have echoed": at the n = 3t+1 boundary the Rule 2
# echo threshold (n+t)/2 + 1 equals the count of honest processes,
# so at local-echo time no readys may exist yet and any honest
# process that missed the bootstrap can leave the cascade one echo
# short forever; only the initiator breaks it.
#
# "held" is the initiator's own withholding (bracha87Fig1Hold): the
# broadcast is not yet out, so there is nothing to retry.  It is not
# a retire -- release (bracha87Fig1Release) reopens the row.  An
# unsent initiator meets neither retire gate -- nothing has echoed or
# readied a broadcast that never went out -- so without "held" the
# row would send it.  A Fig 4 caller with a local coin may
# hold a phase-opening INITIAL until every correct step-3 turn of
# the phase has fired, so no coin is revealed to a process still
# able to force one (bkr94acs.h at bkr94acsBaReveal).
@"retry (initial, v)","am initiator","held","have accepted","all echoed"
no,yes,yes,,
yes,yes,no,no,no
no,yes,no,no,yes
no,yes,no,yes,
no,no,,,

# ===== retry (echo, v) =====
#
# Retry the sent echo iff Bracha is not firing send (echo, v) on
# this dispatch, "have echoed" is set, and this instance has not
# accepted.  The sent value is already in the instance state from
# a prior firing of Rule 1, 2, or 3; BPR does not change it.
@"retry (echo, v)","send (echo, v)","have echoed","have accepted"
no,yes,,
yes,no,yes,no
no,no,yes,yes
no,no,no,

# ===== retry (ready, v) =====
#
# Retry the sent ready iff Bracha is not firing send (ready, v) on
# this dispatch, "have sent ready" is set, and the READY suppress
# mask is short of all n.  "have accepted" does NOT gate this rule.
@"retry (ready, v)","send (ready, v)","have sent ready","READY suppress mask covers all n"
no,yes,,
yes,no,yes,no
no,no,yes,yes
no,no,no,

# ===== record sender accepted =====
#
# A forged one marks only its own sender (BPR.md, Byzantine safety
# of the annotations).
@"record sender accepted","kind of message","carries ACCEPTED"
yes,"(ready, v)",yes
no,"(ready, v)",no
no,"(initial, v)",
no,"(echo, v)",

# ===== arm re-send to sender =====
#
# Chained on accept(v): the accepting message's own arm is taken.
# A marked READY re-arms nothing, which is what keeps the
# exchange from ping-ponging.
@"arm re-send to sender","kind of message","carries RECEIVED","accept(v)","have accepted"
yes,"(ready, v)",no,yes,
yes,"(ready, v)",no,no,yes
no,"(ready, v)",no,no,no
no,"(ready, v)",yes,,
no,"(initial, v)",,,
no,"(echo, v)",,,
